WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.560
Two inhibitions make positive feedback.

2
00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:09.800
But is positive feedback always enough to split two equal cells?

3
00:00:10.833 --> 00:00:16.833
Predict. Give cell A 1 percent more Delta than B. Does the difference always come

4
00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:17.833
back larger?

5
00:00:23.833 --> 00:00:28.413
No. The difference must come back round the loop larger than it left.

6
00:00:29.433 --> 00:00:34.233
Follow it round. B's Notch activity rises, and B's Delta falls.

7
00:00:35.213 --> 00:00:38.173
Then A's Notch activity falls, and A's Delta rises.

8
00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:42.547
Each step passes the change on, multiplied by a factor.

9
00:00:43.347 --> 00:00:45.267
The steeper the step, the bigger the factor.

10
00:00:46.317 --> 00:00:48.537
Multiply the factors all the way round.

11
00:00:49.377 --> 00:00:51.337
That product is the round-trip factor.

12
00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:55.490
Take Collier and colleagues' 1996 model.

13
00:00:56.390 --> 00:01:00.430
With steep responses, the 1 percent comes back as nearly 6

14
00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:03.310
The difference grows, and the pair splits.

15
00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:09.750
With shallow responses, the same 1 percent comes back as about 0.14 percent.

16
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:11.680
The difference fades.

17
00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:14.860
The two cells stay alike, however long you wait.

18
00:01:15.833 --> 00:01:17.553
These numbers come from a model.

19
00:01:18.473 --> 00:01:20.053
They are illustrative, not measurements.

20
00:01:21.117 --> 00:01:24.997
On a steep loop, an even pair is like a pencil balanced on its point.

21
00:01:26.017 --> 00:01:27.397
The slightest tip grows.

22
00:01:28.377 --> 00:01:31.157
There are two stable outcomes: A neural, or B neural.

23
00:01:32.217 --> 00:01:34.577
A system like this is called bistable.

24
00:01:35.377 --> 00:01:39.377
It has two stable outcomes, and the even state between them is unstable.

25
00:01:40.517 --> 00:01:43.197
On a shallow loop, the pair is a marble in a bowl.

26
00:01:44.097 --> 00:01:45.817
Push it, and it rolls back to the middle.

27
00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:49.390
Does the size of the first difference matter much?

28
00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:54.670
In this example, a start ten times smaller delays the split by about a third.

29
00:01:55.733 --> 00:01:58.833
So on a steep loop, ordinary noise can start the split.

30
00:01:59.753 --> 00:02:01.953
Near the threshold, though, the wait grows long.

31
00:02:02.983 --> 00:02:04.463
Now put the cell in a sheet.

32
00:02:05.343 --> 00:02:09.143
It hears the average of its six neighbours, so one neighbour's change is diluted.

33
00:02:10.217 --> 00:02:13.997
In the model, a pair splits when the round-trip factor is above 1.

34
00:02:14.857 --> 00:02:17.417
An ideal hexagonal sheet needs it above 4.

35
00:02:18.417 --> 00:02:23.257
When such a sheet splits from a nearly even start, about one cell in three typically

36
00:02:23.257 --> 00:02:24.257
wins.

37
00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:28.120
That is a spaced pattern, not a guaranteed winner per cluster.

38
00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:32.720
Real tissues add help: a head start, a longer reach, or timing.

39
00:02:34.383 --> 00:02:35.983
The notum has a head start.

40
00:02:36.803 --> 00:02:41.783
Its proneural levels are already uneven, so precursors arise at reproducible spots.

41
00:02:42.867 --> 00:02:44.287
Try the grid in the lesson.

42
00:02:45.247 --> 00:02:47.807
Its feedback scale is its own, not the model's.

43
00:02:48.527 --> 00:02:52.827
Turn the strength up to about 4 or more and step time forward, and a spaced pattern

44
00:02:52.827 --> 00:02:53.827
appears.

45
00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.760
Between two equal cells, the tie breaks only on a steep loop.

46
00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:01.180
A small difference must come back larger than it left.
